# LLM and cloud cost engineering — a task set for p1. THIS FILE IS DATA.
#
# One JSON object per line: {"id", "task", "expectation", "difficulty"}. Only
# "task" is required. "expectation" is a short natural-language success criterion
# the generic rubric scores against; it is NOT an answer key the code matches on,
# and no s15code module reads it for anything but the judge prompt. "difficulty"
# is a free-form label used only to group p1's report — nothing branches on it.
#
# WHY THIS DOMAIN. Every answer is a number that can be checked by hand, so the
# rubric's heaviest criterion (meets_expectation) rests on arithmetic rather than
# on taste. The set also spans difficulty deliberately: if every task is trivial
# the cheapest rung resolves everything and cost-per-resolved-task cannot differ
# from cost-per-call at all, and there is nothing to measure.
#
# HOW THE EXPECTATIONS ARE WRITTEN. Where a plausible wrong answer exists, the
# expectation names it. That is what stops a fluent, confident, wrong answer from
# scoring 4 on all four generic criteria. The traps chosen here are the ones this
# session is itself about:
#
#   averaging two token rates instead of weighting them by token counts   (c05)
#   treating cache reads as free rather than discounted                   (c06)
#   forgetting that retries multiply the number of calls                  (c07)
#   pricing an average output length instead of the full max_tokens       (c09)
#   comparing cost per CALL when the question is cost per RESOLVED task   (c10, c11)
#   counting only the last rung of a cascade, not every rung it paid for  (c12)
#   treating invisible reasoning tokens as unbilled                       (c14)
#   comparing headline input rates when output dominates the bill         (c16)
#
# Every task is self-contained, needs no tool or web access, and has a single
# checkable result.
#
#   python proofs/p1_cost_per_task.py --tasks proofs/tasks/llm_cost_engineering.jsonl --principal you/p2
{"id": "c01_per_million", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "A provider lists $3.00 per million input tokens. What does 12,000 input tokens cost? Give US dollars to four decimal places.", "expectation": "States $0.0360."}
{"id": "c02_one_call", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "A single call uses 1,500 input tokens billed at $0.50 per million and 400 output tokens billed at $1.50 per million. What is the total cost of the call in US dollars, to six decimal places?", "expectation": "States $0.001350."}
{"id": "c03_calls_in_budget", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "A budget of $0.05 is available and every call costs exactly $0.000250. How many whole calls fit inside the budget?", "expectation": "States 200."}
{"id": "c04_rate_ratio", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Model X is billed at $2.00 per million output tokens. Model Y is billed at $0.50 per million output tokens. Y is how many times cheaper per output token? Give a single number.", "expectation": "States 4, meaning four times cheaper. An answer of 75% gives the percentage reduction rather than the ratio that was asked for and fails."}
{"id": "c05_blended_rate", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Every call in a job uses 4,800 input tokens and 900 output tokens. Input is billed at $0.30 per million tokens and output at $2.40 per million tokens. What is the blended cost per million tokens across the job as a whole? Give US dollars to four decimal places.", "expectation": "States approximately $0.6316 per million tokens. An answer of $1.35 averages the two rates without weighting them by the token counts and fails."}
{"id": "c06_cache_discount", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A call sends 10,000 input tokens, of which 9,000 are served from cache and billed at 10% of the normal input rate. The normal input rate is $1.00 per million tokens. The call also produces 500 output tokens billed at $4.00 per million. What is the total cost in US dollars, to six decimal places?", "expectation": "States $0.003900. An answer of $0.003000 treats cached tokens as free rather than discounted and fails."}
{"id": "c07_retry_calls", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A cheap model is retried up to two further times whenever its output is rejected. Across 100 tasks it succeeds on the first attempt for 60 of them; each of the remaining tasks consumes all three attempts and is then abandoned. How many calls does the whole batch make?", "expectation": "States 180 calls."}
{"id": "c08_total_bill", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A provider bills $0.25 per million input tokens and $2.00 per million output tokens. A job makes 2,000 calls, and each call uses 3,000 input tokens and 700 output tokens. What is the total bill in US dollars, to the nearest cent?", "expectation": "States $4.30."}
{"id": "c09_worst_case_projection", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "An admission controller prices the worst case before making a call: it assumes the estimated input tokens plus the FULL max_tokens allowance as output. A tier has max_tokens of 4,096, an estimated 6,000 input tokens, and rates of $0.60 per million input and $3.60 per million output. What projected cost does admission use? Give US dollars to six decimal places.", "expectation": "States approximately $0.018346. An answer that uses an expected or average output length instead of the full max_tokens allowance fails, because a worst-case projection is what makes the ceiling a guarantee."}
{"id": "c10_two_metrics", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Strategy A makes 10 calls at $1.00 each and resolves 10 of 10 tasks. Strategy B makes 20 calls at $0.30 each and resolves 4 of 10 tasks. Give the cost per call and the cost per resolved task for each strategy, and state which strategy is cheaper on each of the two metrics.", "expectation": "Gives A at $1.00 per call and $1.00 per resolved task, B at $0.30 per call and $1.50 per resolved task, and states that B is cheaper per call while A is cheaper per resolved task."}
{"id": "c11_break_even_rate", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Model A costs $2.00 per million input and $8.00 per million output tokens, and resolves every task in a single call. Model B costs $0.15 per million input and $0.75 per million output tokens; a task B fails consumes three calls in total before being abandoned. Every call by either model uses 1,000 input tokens and 300 output tokens. Below what first-attempt resolution rate does B cost more per RESOLVED task than A? Give a percentage to one decimal place.", "expectation": "Arrives at approximately 21.8%. An answer that compares cost per call rather than cost per resolved task fails, as does one that ignores the calls spent on tasks B never resolves."}
{"id": "c12_cascade_pays_every_rung", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A cascade tries the cheapest rung first and escalates one rung whenever a check fails. The rungs cost $0.0004, $0.0026 and $0.0147 per call. On a task where the first two rungs both fail their check and the third answers, what does the cascade spend in total, and how many times more is that than simply accepting the cheapest rung's answer? Give the total to four decimal places and the multiple to two decimal places.", "expectation": "States $0.0177 in total and approximately 44.25 times. An answer that counts only the final rung's $0.0147 ignores that a cascade pays for every rung it passes through and fails."}
{"id": "c13_which_ceiling_binds", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A run is given both a $0.02 lifetime spend ceiling and a 60-call ceiling. Each admitted call costs $0.000127. An adversarial loop requests work 200 times. State how many calls are admitted, how much is spent, and which of the two ceilings binds first, with the reason.", "expectation": "States 60 calls admitted and approximately $0.00762 spent, and identifies the CALL ceiling as binding first because the spend ceiling alone would have allowed roughly 157 calls."}
{"id": "c14_invisible_tokens", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A model is called with max_tokens of 512. With its reasoning channel enabled it produces 445 output tokens per call, of which only 95 appear in the visible answer; the remainder are billed but never seen. With reasoning disabled it produces 95 output tokens, all visible. Output is billed at $3.60 per million tokens. Across 1,000 calls, how much is spent on invisible tokens, and how many times more expensive is the reasoning-enabled configuration on output alone? Give the amount to two decimal places and the multiple to two decimal places.", "expectation": "States approximately $1.26 spent on invisible tokens across 1,000 calls and approximately 4.68 times more expensive on output. An answer that treats the invisible reasoning tokens as unbilled fails."}
{"id": "c15_mixed_routing", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A workload of 1,000 requests is routed so that 70% go to a rung costing $0.00040 per call, 20% to a rung costing $0.00260, and 10% to a rung costing $0.01470. Give the total cost of the workload and the mean cost per request, both in US dollars to four decimal places.", "expectation": "States $2.2700 in total and approximately $0.0023 per request."}
{"id": "c16_crossover_point", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Model P costs $0.15 per million input tokens and $0.75 per million output tokens. Model Q costs $0.30 per million input and $0.25 per million output. For a call with 2,000 input tokens, above how many output tokens does Q become cheaper than P? Give a whole number of output tokens.", "expectation": "States 600 output tokens, above which Q is cheaper. An answer that compares only the headline input rates concludes P is always cheaper and fails, because output rates dominate once the answer is long enough."}
{"id": "c17_judge_meta_cost", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Answering a 12-task set costs $0.00213675 across 12 calls. Grading those same answers takes 26 judge calls consuming 26,339 input tokens and 1,916 output tokens, billed at $0.50 per million for both input and output. What does the judging cost, and what multiple of the answering spend is it? Give the cost to eight decimal places and the multiple to one decimal place.", "expectation": "States $0.01412750 for judging and approximately 6.6 times the answering spend."}
